$article=['date'=>'2026-09-28','slug'=>'developmental-milestones-by-age','tag'=>'Child Development','category'=>'Parenting & Child Development','breadcrumb'=>'Developmental Milestones by Age','meta_title'=>'Developmental Milestones by Age: A Practical Guide','description'=>'Check developmental milestones by age across communication, movement, learning and social skills, then use our free tool to review your child&rsquo;s progress.','h1'=>'Developmental Milestones by Age: A Practical Guide','dek'=>'A milestone guide should help you observe your child and start useful conversations, not turn childhood into a monthly examination.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Developmental milestones by age describe skills most children can do by a certain point in how they play, learn, communicate, behave and move. They are monitoring tools, not diagnostic tests. One missed item does not explain why a child is developing differently.</p>
<h2>What developmental milestones can tell you</h2>
<p>Milestones help families notice patterns and changes. The CDC&rsquo;s current checklists place milestones at ages when at least 75 percent of children would be expected to show them. A child can reach some early and others later.</p>
<p>Look across domains rather than focusing only on speech or walking. Communication, social connection, problem-solving, fine motor and gross motor skills develop together but not always at the same pace.</p>
<h2>Birth to 12 months</h2>
<p>During the first year, babies increasingly respond to people and sounds, take turns making noises, explore objects and gain control of their bodies. By around 12 months, many play an interactive game, call a parent by a special name, put an object into a container and pull to stand.</p>
<p>Watch how your baby communicates before words. Looking, reaching, showing, babbling and copying are meaningful. Limited response to sound should prompt a hearing conversation.</p>
<h2>From 12 to 24 months</h2>
<p>Between one and two, children become more intentional. By around 18 months, many point to show something interesting, follow a simple direction, try several words, walk independently and copy a familiar household action.</p>
<p>By two, many put two words together, look at a caregiver&rsquo;s face in a new situation, run, kick a ball and use more than one toy in a connected play sequence. Observe understanding and gestures as well as spoken vocabulary.</p>
<h2>Ages two to three</h2>
<p>Language, pretend play and independence expand quickly. Many children begin short back-and-forth conversations, ask simple questions, join other children in play, draw a circle after a demonstration and manage more dressing and eating tasks.</p>
<p>Frustration and uneven skills remain common. Context matters: a tired child in an unfamiliar clinic may show less than they do at home.</p>
<h2>Ages three to five</h2>
<p>Children usually develop longer conversations, more complex pretend play, growing awareness of other people and greater control of movement and self-care. By five, many follow rules or take turns in games, recognise simple rhymes, write some letters in their name and hop on one foot.</p>
<h2>Normal variation or reason for concern?</h2>
<p>Ask three questions. Is your child continuing to gain skills? Are differences limited to one area or spread across several? Do they interfere with communicating, playing, learning or daily participation?</p>
<p>Loss of a skill should be discussed promptly. So should a cluster of missed milestones, limited progress or a parent&rsquo;s persistent concern. Screening gives a more structured view than a milestone checklist and may lead to assessment.</p>
<h2>Use a checker without letting it label your child</h2>
<p>Complete the <a href="/resources/child-milestone-checker">free child milestone checker</a> based on what your child usually does, not their best day or a pressured test. Save examples and take the results to a paediatrician if concerns appear.</p>
<div class="safety-card"><p><strong>Remember:</strong> a milestone checklist cannot diagnose autism, ADHD, language disorder or another condition. It helps identify which observations may deserve professional discussion.</p></div>
<h2>What happens after a concern?</h2>
<p>A paediatrician may review health and development, check hearing and vision, use a validated screener or refer to a developmental professional. Support can often address an identified need while diagnostic questions are still being considered.</p>
